Cognitive Field Computing:

Alignment-Constrained Fractional Dynamics on Hybrid Information Manifolds

ouadi Maakoul 

1. Introduction and Scope of This Revision

We present a corrected mathematical formulation of Cognitive Field Computing (CFC). The central object is a non-negative density field \Psi(\mathbf{x},\theta,t) on a product domain \Omega\times\Theta, evolving under a time-fractional reaction–diffusion equation with Caputo derivative of order \alpha\in(0,1), with alignment enforced by a smoothed projection acting on the normalized belief distribution at each spatial point.

The present revision makes four classes of correction relative to earlier formulations.

First, the fractional Lyapunov inequality of Gomoyunov (2017) is retained as the correct tool replacing the invalid fractional chain rule, but its applicability to the alignment functional is now stated honestly: the alignment functional is not convex in \Psi, and the fractional Lyapunov inequality therefore does not apply to it directly. We supply an explicit counterexample and reformulate alignment invariance as a conditional result resting on an explicit gradient-domination (Łojasiewicz-type) hypothesis.

Second, the dissipativity of the diffusion term against the alignment functional is reformulated. The pointwise chain-rule identity \nabla(\delta\mathcal{A}/\delta\Psi)=(\delta^2\mathcal{A}/\delta\Psi^2)\nabla\Psi used in earlier revisions is invalid for the nonlocal functional derivative of \mathcal{A}. We state the diffusion-dissipation estimate as an explicit hypothesis and prove a partial result under a spatial-locality assumption on the reference field.

Third, the functional derivative of \mathcal{A}, the sign convention in the reaction entropy production, and the Kleisli composition bound are retained from the previous revision, as these corrections are valid. The regularization \mathcal{A}\to\mathcal{A}_\mu is handled consistently: all structural results are proved for \mathcal{A}_\mu, and we state explicitly which constants depend on \mu.

Fourth, the status of the Hilbert-space fractional Lyapunov inequality, the gradient-domination hypothesis, the spectral hypotheses underlying geometric coherence, and the fixed-point argument for the coupled system are all stated with their precise dependencies.

We do not claim an unconditional alignment-invariance theorem. The honest status of the main results is summarized in §12.

2. Notation and Standing Assumptions

Domain. \Omega\subset\mathbb{R}^d is a bounded open set with smooth boundary \partial\Omega. \Theta is a Polish space equipped with a reference measure \mu_\Theta, typically a compact subset of \mathbb{R}^m.

Time horizon. T\in(0,\infty) is fixed.

Fractional order. \alpha\in(0,1) is fixed.

Function spaces. H=L^2(\Omega\times\Theta;\mu_\Omega\otimes\mu_\Theta). H^1=H^1(\Omega\times\Theta). H^2_N is H^2(\Omega\times\Theta) with Neumann boundary conditions. C([0,T];X) is the space of continuous X-valued functions; L^p(0,T;X) is the usual Bochner space. \mathcal{P}(\Theta) denotes Borel probability measures.

Field. \Psi:\Omega\times\Theta\times[0,T]\to\mathbb{R}_{\ge 0} is the intelligence density field. The normalized belief distribution at (\mathbf{x},t) is

p_{\mathbf{x},t}(\theta)=\frac{\Psi(\mathbf{x},\theta,t)}{\rho(\mathbf{x},t)},\qquad \rho(\mathbf{x},t):=\int_\Theta\Psi(\mathbf{x},\theta,t)\,d\mu_\Theta(\theta),

defined whenever \rho(\mathbf{x},t)>0.

Target distribution. \mathcal{V}\in\mathcal{P}(\Theta) is a fixed reference distribution, absolutely continuous with respect to \mu_\Theta, with density also denoted \mathcal{V}.

Standing assumptions.

(SA1) \Psi_0\in H^1\cap L^\infty, \Psi_0\ge 0, and \rho_0(\mathbf{x}):=\int_\Theta\Psi_0\,d\mu_\Theta\ge\delta_{\min}>0.

(SA2) \mathcal{V} has a positive continuous density with \mathcal{V}(\theta)\ge\nu_{\min}>0 on \Theta.

(SA3) The diffusion coefficient D depends on \Psi through a bounded Lipschitz observable \sigma. Precisely, D(\mathbf{x},\theta,t)=\Phi(\sigma(\mathbf{x},\theta,t)) with \Phi:\mathbb{R}\to[D_{\min},D_{\max}] Lipschitz (0<D_{\min}\le D_{\max}<\infty), and \sigma:[0,T]\to H^s(\Omega\times\Theta) Lipschitz from H^1 to H^s on bounded subsets, for some s>d/2. This ensures A(\Psi)=-\nabla\cdot(D(\sigma)\nabla\cdot) depends Lipschitz-continuously on \Psi in the interpolation space H^1 and that H^s\hookrightarrow L^\infty.

(SA4) (Gradient-domination / Łojasiewicz hypothesis.) There exists c_{\mathrm{L}}>0 and \beta\in(0,1) such that, for the regularized alignment functional \mathcal{A}_\mu of §3.2,

\left\|\frac{\delta\mathcal{A}_\mu}{\delta\Psi}\right\|_{L^2}^2\;\ge\;c_{\mathrm{L}}\,\bigl(\mathcal{A}_\mu(\Psi,\mathcal{V})-\epsilon\bigr)_+^{2\beta}.

This replaces the false convexity assumption used in earlier revisions. Its verification is discussed in §3.5.

(SA5) (Diffusion-dissipation hypothesis.) There exists \kappa_{\mathrm{d}}\ge 0 such that

\left\langle\frac{\delta\mathcal{A}_\mu}{\delta\Psi},\,\nabla\cdot(D\nabla\Psi)\right\rangle\;\le\;\kappa_{\mathrm{d}}\,\left\|\frac{\delta\mathcal{A}_\mu}{\delta\Psi}\right\|_{L^2}^2

for all \Psi in the regularity class of Theorem 4.1. The case \kappa_{\mathrm{d}}=0 corresponds to unconditional dissipativity; we discuss in §3.6 the partial result that justifies a strictly positive but controlled \kappa_{\mathrm{d}}.

Remark 2.1. (SA4) is a Łojasiewicz–Simon-type inequality and, for KL-type functionals, is closely related to a logarithmic Sobolev inequality for the reference measure \mathcal{V}. It is not automatic from (SA1)–(SA2) and must be imposed. (SA5) is discussed in detail in §3.6.

3. The Field Equation with Alignment Constraint

3.1 Equation of Motion

Let \alpha\in(0,1]. Let D satisfy (SA3) and let F_{\mathrm{BUDT}}:H^1\times\mathcal{P}(\Theta)\times\mathcal{M}\to H be the resource-constrained active inference reaction term. The field evolves according to

{}^C_0D_t^\alpha\Psi=\nabla\cdot(D\nabla\Psi)+F_{\mathrm{BUDT}}(\Psi,S,M;\mathbf{C},\mathbf{U})+\Lambda_{\mathcal{A},k}(\Psi),
\tag{3.1}

with Neumann boundary condition \partial_{\mathbf{n}}\Psi=0 on \partial\Omega\times\Theta and initial condition \Psi(\mathbf{x},\theta,0)=\Psi_0(\mathbf{x},\theta).

3.2 Alignment Functional and Regularization

Definition 3.1 (Alignment functional). For \Psi with \rho(\mathbf{x},t)>0,

\mathcal{A}(\Psi,\mathcal{V})=\int_\Omega D_{\mathrm{KL}}\!\left(p_{\mathbf{x},t}\,\Big\|\,\mathcal{V}\right)w(\mathbf{x})\,d\mathbf{x},
\tag{3.2}

where p_{\mathbf{x},t}(\theta)=\Psi(\mathbf{x},\theta,t)/\rho(\mathbf{x},t), w\in C^\infty(\overline\Omega) is non-negative with \int_\Omega w=1, and

D_{\mathrm{KL}}(p\|\mathcal{V})=\int_\Theta p(\theta)\log\frac{p(\theta)}{\mathcal{V}(\theta)}\,d\mu_\Theta(\theta).

Definition 3.2 (Regularized alignment functional). For a mollifier \mu\in L^\infty_+(\Theta) with \mu\ge\mu_{\min}>0, define

\mathcal{A}_\mu(\Psi,\mathcal{V})=\int_\Omega D_{\mathrm{KL}}\!\left(\frac{\Psi+\mu}{\|\Psi+\mu\|_1}\,\Big\|\,\frac{\mathcal{V}+\mu}{\|\mathcal{V}+\mu\|_1}\right)w(\mathbf{x})\,d\mathbf{x}.
\tag{3.3}

The regularized functional is finite for all \Psi\in H^1 with \Psi\ge 0, and \mathcal{A}_\mu\to\mathcal{A} as \mu\to 0 on the set \{\Psi\ge\delta_{\min}\}.

Definition 3.3 (Smoothed alignment projection). For tolerance \epsilon>0, gain \eta>0, and smoothing parameter k>0, define

\Lambda_{\mathcal{A},k}(\Psi)=-\eta\,\nabla_\Psi\sigma_k\!\left(\mathcal{A}_\mu(\Psi,\mathcal{V})-\epsilon\right),
\tag{3.4}

where \sigma_k(z)=\tfrac{1}{k}\log(1+e^{kz}) is the softplus function. The map \Lambda_{\mathcal{A},k}:H^1\to H is C^1 and Lipschitz on bounded sets.

3.3 Functional Derivative of the Alignment Functional

Lemma 3.4. Let \mathcal{A}_\mu be given by (3.3) with \Psi\in H^1, \Psi\ge 0. Then for every (\mathbf{x},\theta)\in\Omega\times\Theta,

\frac{\delta\mathcal{A}_\mu}{\delta\Psi}(\mathbf{x},\theta)=\frac{w(\mathbf{x})}{\rho_\mu(\mathbf{x})}\left[\log\frac{p_{\mathbf{x},t}^{(\mu)}(\theta)}{\mathcal{V}_\mu(\theta)}-D_{\mathrm{KL}}\!\left(p_{\mathbf{x},t}^{(\mu)}\,\Big\|\,\mathcal{V}_\mu\right)\right],
\tag{3.5}

where \rho_\mu(\mathbf{x})=\int_\Theta(\Psi+\mu)\,d\mu_\Theta, p_{\mathbf{x},t}^{(\mu)}=(\Psi+\mu)/\rho_\mu, and \mathcal{V}_\mu=(\mathcal{V}+\mu)/\|\mathcal{V}+\mu\|_1.

Proof. Direct computation of the first variation. The +\!1 term inside the logarithm that appears in a naive computation cancels against the normalization contribution from \rho_\mu; see the consistency check that \delta\mathcal{A}_\mu/\delta\Psi=0 when p^{(\mu)}_{\mathbf{x},t}=\mathcal{V}_\mu. \square

3.4 Non-Convexity of the Alignment Functional in \Psi

Earlier revisions asserted that \mathcal{A} (and \mathcal{A}_\mu) is convex in \Psi on H^1. This is false.

Proposition 3.5 (Non-convexity). Let \Theta=\{1,2\}, \mu_\Theta the counting measure, \mathcal{V} uniform, and w\equiv 1 on \Omega. Write \Psi=(a,b) on a single spatial point. Then

\mathcal{A}(a,b)=\log 2+\frac{a\log a+b\log b}{a+b}-\log(a+b).

At (a,b)=(2,1), the Hessian

H=\begin{pmatrix}0.0043&-0.0854\\-0.0854&0.3249\end{pmatrix}

has negative determinant, so \mathcal{A} is not convex.

Proof. Direct computation. The map \Psi\mapsto p=\Psi/\rho is homogeneous of degree zero, not affine, and composition of a degree-zero homogeneous map with the convex KL divergence does not preserve convexity. \square

Consequence. The fractional Lyapunov inequality, which applies to convex functionals, does not apply to \mathcal{A} or \mathcal{A}_\mu directly. The argument of earlier revisions that eliminated Hypothesis (H-AI) is therefore invalid.

3.5 Reformulation of Alignment Invariance

Because \mathcal{A}_\mu is not convex, we cannot use the fractional Lyapunov inequality as a black box. We instead work with the differential inequality obtained directly from the chain rule for Caputo derivatives. For \mathcal{A}_\mu sufficiently regular in time, the fractional Leibniz rule gives

{}^C_0D_t^\alpha\mathcal{A}_\mu(\Psi(t),\mathcal{V})
=\left\langle\frac{\delta\mathcal{A}_\mu}{\delta\Psi}(\Psi(t)),\,{}^C_0D_t^\alpha\Psi(t)\right\rangle+R_\mu(t),
\tag{3.6}

where R_\mu(t) is the fractional Leibniz remainder. For convex functionals, R_\mu\le 0 is the content of the Gomoyunov inequality. For non-convex functionals the sign of R_\mu is not determined in general. We therefore make the following explicit hypothesis.

(H-REM) (Fractional Leibniz remainder.) The remainder R_\mu in (3.6) satisfies R_\mu(t)\le 0 for a.e. t\in(0,T) along solutions of (3.1) in the regularity class of Theorem 4.1.

Remark 3.6. (H-REM) is a genuine hypothesis. It is not a consequence of (SA1)–(SA5). Its verification for the specific functional \mathcal{A}_\mu is an open problem; see §12. It replaces, but does not eliminate, Hypothesis (H-AI) of earlier revisions.

Substituting (3.1) into (3.6) and using (H-REM),

{}^C_0D_t^\alpha\mathcal{A}_\mu
\le\left\langle\frac{\delta\mathcal{A}_\mu}{\delta\Psi},\,\nabla\cdot(D\nabla\Psi)\right\rangle+\left\langle\frac{\delta\mathcal{A}_\mu}{\delta\Psi},\,F_{\mathrm{BUDT}}\right\rangle+\left\langle\frac{\delta\mathcal{A}_\mu}{\delta\Psi},\,\Lambda_{\mathcal{A},k}\right\rangle.
\tag{3.7}

The three terms are handled as follows.

(a) Diffusion term. Under (SA5),

\left\langle\frac{\delta\mathcal{A}_\mu}{\delta\Psi},\,\nabla\cdot(D\nabla\Psi)\right\rangle\le\kappa_{\mathrm{d}}\left\|\frac{\delta\mathcal{A}_\mu}{\delta\Psi}\right\|_{L^2}^2.
\tag{3.8}

(b) Reaction term. We impose the alignment-compatibility condition

\left\langle\frac{\delta\mathcal{A}_\mu}{\delta\Psi},\,F_{\mathrm{BUDT}}(\Psi)\right\rangle\le 0\quad\text{on }\{\mathcal{A}_\mu>\epsilon\}.
\tag{3.9}

(c) Projection term. By the chain rule for \Lambda_{\mathcal{A},k}=-\eta\,\sigma_k'(\mathcal{A}_\mu-\epsilon)\,\delta\mathcal{A}_\mu/\delta\Psi,

\left\langle\frac{\delta\mathcal{A}_\mu}{\delta\Psi},\,\Lambda_{\mathcal{A},k}\right\rangle=-\eta\,\sigma_k'(\mathcal{A}_\mu-\epsilon)\left\|\frac{\delta\mathcal{A}_\mu}{\delta\Psi}\right\|_{L^2}^2\le-c_0\left\|\frac{\delta\mathcal{A}_\mu}{\delta\Psi}\right\|_{L^2}^2,
\tag{3.10}

with c_0=\eta/2 on \{\mathcal{A}_\mu>\epsilon\}.

Combining (3.7)–(3.10),

{}^C_0D_t^\alpha\mathcal{A}_\mu\le-(c_0-\kappa_{\mathrm{d}})\left\|\frac{\delta\mathcal{A}_\mu}{\delta\Psi}\right\|_{L^2}^2.
\tag{3.11}

Under (SA4), \|\delta\mathcal{A}_\mu/\delta\Psi\|^2\ge c_{\mathrm{L}}(\mathcal{A}_\mu-\epsilon)_+^{2\beta}, so

{}^C_0D_t^\alpha\mathcal{A}_\mu\le-c\,(\mathcal{A}_\mu-\epsilon)_+^{2\beta}
\tag{3.12}

on \{\mathcal{A}_\mu>\epsilon\}, where c=(c_0-\kappa_{\mathrm{d}})c_{\mathrm{L}}>0 provided \eta is chosen large enough relative to \kappa_{\mathrm{d}}.

Proposition 3.7 (Alignment invariance; conditional). Suppose (SA1)–(SA5), (H-REM), the alignment-compatibility condition (3.9), and \eta>2\kappa_{\mathrm{d}}. If \mathcal{A}_\mu(\Psi_0,\mathcal{V})\le\epsilon, then \mathcal{A}_\mu(\Psi(t),\mathcal{V})\le\epsilon for all t\in[0,T].

Proof. Apply the fractional comparison principle (Lemma 2.3 below) to (3.12) with the constant function y(t)\equiv\epsilon: on \{\mathcal{A}_\mu>\epsilon\}, {}^C_0D_t^\alpha y=0\ge-c(\mathcal{A}_\mu-\epsilon)_+^{2\beta}, and y(0)=\epsilon\ge\mathcal{A}_\mu(\Psi_0,\mathcal{V}). The comparison principle then gives \mathcal{A}_\mu(\Psi(t),\mathcal{V})\le\epsilon. \square

Remark 3.8. Proposition 3.7 is conditional on (H-REM) and (SA5). Both are structural hypotheses. (H-REM) is not automatic for non-convex \mathcal{A}_\mu; (SA5) is discussed below.

3.6 The Diffusion-Dissipation Hypothesis (SA5)

Earlier revisions asserted, via an invalid pointwise chain rule, that

\left\langle\frac{\delta\mathcal{A}}{\delta\Psi},\,\nabla\cdot(D\nabla\Psi)\right\rangle=-\int D\left|\nabla\frac{\delta\mathcal{A}}{\delta\Psi}\right|^2\le 0.

The chain rule \nabla(\delta\mathcal{A}/\delta\Psi)=(\delta^2\mathcal{A}/\delta\Psi^2)\nabla\Psi is invalid: \delta\mathcal{A}/\delta\Psi(\mathbf{x},\theta) depends on \Psi nonlocally through \rho(\mathbf{x}) and through the KL integral at \mathbf{x}. The second variation is an integral kernel, not a pointwise multiplication operator.

The correct expression is

\left\langle\frac{\delta\mathcal{A}_\mu}{\delta\Psi},\,\nabla\cdot(D\nabla\Psi)\right\rangle=-\int_{\Omega\times\Theta}D\,\nabla\Psi\cdot\nabla\!\left(\frac{\delta\mathcal{A}_\mu}{\delta\Psi}\right)d(\mathbf{x},\theta).

The sign of this quantity is not determined by convexity alone. We therefore impose (SA5) as a hypothesis.

Partial result. Under the additional assumption that the reference distribution \mathcal{V} is constant in \theta and w\equiv\text{const}, one can compute

\nabla\!\left(\frac{\delta\mathcal{A}_\mu}{\delta\Psi}\right)=\frac{w}{\rho_\mu}\left[\frac{\nabla\Psi}{\Psi+\mu}-\frac{\nabla\rho_\mu}{\rho_\mu}\log\frac{p^{(\mu)}}{\mathcal{V}_\mu}\right]-\frac{w\nabla\rho_\mu}{\rho_\mu^2}\left[\log\frac{p^{(\mu)}}{\mathcal{V}_\mu}-D_{\mathrm{KL}}\right],

and the inner product with \nabla\Psi is not sign-definite. This confirms that (SA5) is a genuine restriction; it is not a consequence of (SA1)–(SA4).

4. Well-Posedness

4.1 Fractional Operators and Solution Operators

For 0<\alpha<1 and f\in AC([0,T]), the Caputo derivative is

{}^C_0D_t^\alpha f(t)=\frac{1}{\Gamma(1-\alpha)}\int_0^t\frac{f'(s)}{(t-s)^\alpha}\,ds,

and the Riemann–Liouville integral is {}_0I_t^\alpha f(t)=\frac{1}{\Gamma(\alpha)}\int_0^t(t-s)^{\alpha-1}f(s)\,ds.

For a sectorial operator A on a Banach space X, the Mittag-Leffler solution operator is

E_\alpha(t^\alpha A)=\frac{1}{2\pi i}\int_\Gamma e^{\lambda t}(\lambda^\alpha-A)^{-1}d\lambda.

The family \{E_\alpha(t^\alpha A)\}_{t\ge 0} is a solution operator family for {}^C_0D_t^\alpha u=Au, u(0)=u_0, with \|E_\alpha(t^\alpha A)\|_{L(X)}\le Me^{\omega t}, but is not a semigroup: E_\alpha((t+s)^\alpha A)\ne E_\alpha(t^\alpha A)E_\alpha(s^\alpha A) in general.

4.2 Fractional Comparison Principle

The classical chain rule fails for Caputo derivatives. The correct tool for Lyapunov analysis is the fractional comparison principle.

Lemma 4.1. Let x\in AC([0,T]) with {}^C_0D_t^\alpha x\in L^1(0,T), and let f:[0,T]\times\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R} be continuous and Lipschitz in its second argument. Suppose y\in AC([0,T]) satisfies {}^C_0D_t^\alpha y(t)\ge f(t,y(t)) for a.e. t\in[0,T] and y(0)\ge x(0), while x satisfies {}^C_0D_t^\alpha x(t)=f(t,x(t)). Then x(t)\le y(t) for all t\in[0,T].

Corollary 4.2 (Sublevel-set invariance). Let \epsilon>0 and c>0. If x(0)\le\epsilon and

{}^C_0D_t^\alpha x(t)\le-c\,(x(t)-\epsilon)_+\quad\text{for a.e. }t\in(0,T),

then x(t)\le\epsilon for all t\in[0,T].

Proof. The right-hand side f(t,y)=-c(y-\epsilon)_+ is Lipschitz in y. The constant function y(t)\equiv\epsilon satisfies {}^C_0D_t^\alpha y=0\ge f(t,\epsilon)=0, and y(0)=\epsilon\ge x(0). Lemma 4.1 gives x(t)\le\epsilon. \square

4.3 Maximal Regularity for Quasilinear Fractional Problems

We use the theory of maximal regularity for quasilinear subdiffusion problems in the sense of Zacher (2012), with the interpolation-space framework of Clément–Londen–Simonett (2004) applied to the associated parabolic problem.

Theorem 4.3. Let X_0,X_1 be Banach spaces with X_1\hookrightarrow X_0 densely. Let A(\cdot):X_1\to X_0 be a family of closed operators such that -A(u) generates an analytic semigroup on X_0 for each u\in X_1, uniformly sectorial. Consider

{}^C_0D_t^\alpha(u-u_0)+A(u)u=f(u)+h(t),\qquad u(0)=u_0.

Under Lipschitz and continuity assumptions on A(\cdot) and f(\cdot) in the interpolation space X_\gamma=(X_0,X_1)_{\gamma,\infty} for suitable \gamma, there exist T_0>0 and a unique solution u\in C([0,T_0];X_\gamma)\cap C((0,T_0];X_1) with {}^C_0D_t^\alpha u\in C((0,T_0];X_0). If the solution remains bounded in X_\gamma for all finite times, it extends globally.

Application. Take X_0=L^2(\Omega\times\Theta), X_1=H^2_N(\Omega\times\Theta), X_\gamma=H^1, and A(\Psi)=-\nabla\cdot(D(\sigma)\nabla\cdot). Under (SA3), A(\Psi) is sectorial with uniform constants D_{\min}\le D\le D_{\max} and Lipschitz in \Psi on bounded subsets of H^1.

4.4 Local and Global Existence

Theorem 4.4 (Local existence). Let (SA1)–(SA3) hold. Suppose F_{\mathrm{BUDT}}:H^1\to H and \Lambda_{\mathcal{A},k}:H^1\to H are Lipschitz on bounded sets, and D satisfies (SA3). Then there exist T_0>0 and a unique solution \Psi\in C([0,T_0];H^1)\cap C((0,T_0];H^2_N) with {}^C_0D_t^\alpha\Psi\in C((0,T_0];L^2) of (3.1), \Psi(0)=\Psi_0.

Theorem 4.5 (Global existence). Under the hypotheses of Theorem 4.4, if F_{\mathrm{BUDT}} satisfies the dissipativity conditions

\langle\Psi,F_{\mathrm{BUDT}}(\Psi)\rangle\le C_1\|\Psi\|_{L^2}^2+C_2,\qquad
\langle\nabla\Psi,\nabla F_{\mathrm{BUDT}}(\Psi)\rangle\le C_4\|\nabla\Psi\|_{L^2}^2+C_5,

and \langle\Psi,\Lambda_{\mathcal{A},k}(\Psi)\rangle\le C_3, then the local solution extends to [0,T] for every T<\infty.

5. Thermodynamic Consistency

5.1 Gradient Structure on the Reaction Term

(H-TH) There exists a convex, coercive functional \mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{BUDT}}:H^1\to\mathbb{R}, C^1, with unique minimizer \Psi^*, such that

F_{\mathrm{BUDT}}(\Psi)=-\nabla_\Psi\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{BUDT}}(\Psi).
\tag{5.1}

This is a structural assumption on the active inference term, standard near equilibrium.

5.2 Entropy Production

Following the geometric decomposition of entropy production for out-of-equilibrium systems (Kolchinsky–Ito, 2022, for discrete Markov jump systems), we adapt the decomposition to the present field-theoretic setting by analogy. Define

\sigma=\sigma_{\mathrm{diff}}+\sigma_{\mathrm{react}}+\sigma_{\mathrm{cross}},
\tag{5.2}

where

\sigma_{\mathrm{diff}}=\frac{D}{T}|\nabla\Psi|^2\ge 0,\qquad
\sigma_{\mathrm{react}}=-\frac{1}{T}\left\langle F_{\mathrm{BUDT}}(\Psi)-F_{\mathrm{BUDT}}(\Psi^*),\Psi-\Psi^*\right\rangle.
\tag{5.3}

Under (H-TH), F_{\mathrm{BUDT}}(\Psi)-F_{\mathrm{BUDT}}(\Psi^*)=-\nabla\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{BUDT}}(\Psi)+\nabla\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{BUDT}}(\Psi^*), so

\sigma_{\mathrm{react}}=\frac{1}{T}\left\langle\nabla\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{BUDT}}(\Psi)-\nabla\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{BUDT}}(\Psi^*),\Psi-\Psi^*\right\rangle\ge 0
\tag{5.4}

by monotonicity of the gradient of a convex functional. The cross term \sigma_{\mathrm{cross}} vanishes when thermodynamic forces are orthogonal.

Remark 5.1. The Kolchinsky–Ito decomposition is stated for discrete Markov jump systems and does not contain reaction–diffusion PDE formulas; the adaptation to the present field-theoretic setting is by analogy and should be regarded as a modeling choice, not a derived result.

6. Information Geometry of the Cognitive Manifold

6.1 Common Manifold Construction

The Fisher–Rao metric g^{\mathrm{Fisher}} and the Wasserstein–Fisher–Rao metric g^{\mathrm{WFR}} are not automatically defined on the same manifold. We place them on a common manifold as follows.

Construction 6.1. Let \mathcal{M}\subset\mathcal{P}(\Theta) be a parameterized family \{p_\theta:\theta\in\Theta_{\mathcal{M}}\} with \Theta_{\mathcal{M}}\subset\mathbb{R}^m open, such that p_\theta is C^1 in \theta and positive on \Theta, g^{\mathrm{Fisher}}_{ij}(\theta)=\int_\Theta\partial_i\log p_\theta\,\partial_j\log p_\theta\,p_\theta\,d\mu_\Theta is well-defined, and the WFR metric restricted to \mathcal{M} is well-defined and positive-definite.

(H-WFR) The WFR metric on \mathcal{M} is well-defined, positive-definite, and smooth in \theta.

On \mathcal{M}, define the hybrid metric

g^{\mathrm{hyb}}_{ij}(\theta)=(1-\lambda)g^{\mathrm{Fisher}}_{ij}(\theta)+\lambda g^{\mathrm{WFR}}_{ij}(\theta),\qquad\lambda\in[0,1].
\tag{6.1}

Proposition 6.2. Under (H-WFR) and \lambda>0, g^{\mathrm{hyb}} is positive-definite on \mathcal{M}, even where g^{\mathrm{Fisher}} degenerates.

The natural gradient flow is \dot{\theta}=-(g^{\mathrm{hyb}})^{-1}\nabla_\theta\mathcal{F}(\theta;o), locally well-posed by Picard–Lindelöf.

7. Categorical Compositionality

7.1 Alignment-Enriched Kleisli Category

Let \mathcal{P} be the Giry monad on \mathbf{Meas}. An alignment-enriched cognitive module is a pair (k,\alpha) where k:X\to\mathcal{P}(Y) is a Markov kernel and \alpha:X\to\mathbb{R}_{\ge 0} is measurable with D_{\mathrm{KL}}(k(\cdot|x)\|\mathcal{V}_Y)\le\alpha(x) for all x\in X. The composite of (k,\alpha):X\to Y and (\ell,\beta):Y\to Z is (\ell\circ_K k,\gamma) with (\ell\circ_K k)(dz|x)=\int_Y\ell(dz|y)k(dy|x) and \gamma(x)=\int_Y\beta(y)k(dy|x).

Theorem 7.1. If (k,\alpha) and (\ell,\beta) are alignment-enriched modules, then

D_{\mathrm{KL}}((\ell\circ_K k)(\cdot|x)\|\mathcal{V}_Z)\le\gamma(x)
\tag{7.1}

for all x\in X.

Proof. By convexity of the KL divergence in its first argument (Jensen's inequality), for q(dz|x)=\int_Y\ell(dz|y)k(dy|x),

D_{\mathrm{KL}}(q\|\mathcal{V}_Z)\le\int_Y D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\ell(\cdot|y)\|\mathcal{V}_Z)\,k(dy|x)\le\int_Y\beta(y)k(dy|x)=\gamma(x).

\square

Remark 7.2. The previous revision used a non-standard "chain rule" for KL divergence. The correct estimate is the convexity (Jensen) inequality above; the resulting composition bound is strictly stronger.

8. Mean-Field Games with Adversarial Agents

We consider N agents, fraction \rho adversarial, with reputation-aware mean-field game dynamics as in the federated-learning template of Sun–Wu–Li (2024). The reputation variable r^i\in[0,1] is updated by r^i_{t+1}=r^i_t+\kappa(\cos(a^i_t,\bar{a}_t)-r^i_t), and agents with reputation below threshold \theta_r are excluded from the field update.

Remark 8.1. The FedMFG work is a federated-learning systems paper. It does not contain a Nash-equilibrium-in-Lipschitz-controls theorem, a spectral-gap analysis of the linearized FP operator, or the critical-fraction formula \rho_c. The statements below are therefore conditional on a framework that must be supplied separately; they are not consequences of the cited source.

Conditional claim. Under a reputation-aware mean-field game framework satisfying the required equilibrium and spectral hypotheses, if

\rho<\rho_c=\frac{1}{2}\left(1-\frac{\kappa}{\kappa+\lambda_{\min}(D)}\right),
\tag{8.1}

then the alignment divergence at the NESS satisfies \mathcal{A}(\Psi^*,\mathcal{V})\le\epsilon+C\rho for a constant C depending on \theta_r and the spectral gap of the linearized operator. The derivation of (8.1) requires the spectral analysis of the linearized coupled FP–HJB system, which is not established here.

9. Geometric Coherence

Definition 9.1 (Geometric coherence). A field \Psi is geometrically coherent with parameters (\gamma_0,K_0,\delta_0) if:

1. (Spectral gap.) The linearization of (3.1) around \Psi has spectral gap \gamma(\Psi)\ge\gamma_0>0 in L^2(\Omega\times\Theta).
2. (Curvature bound.) \mathrm{Ric}(g^{\mathrm{hyb}})\ge-K_0 on \mathcal{M}.
3. (Basin stability.) The attractor \mathcal{A}^* has basin of attraction with volume \ge\delta_0.
4. (Alignment compatibility.) \phi(\Psi)=\langle\nabla_\Psi\mathcal{C}(\Psi),\nabla_\Psi\mathcal{A}(\Psi,\mathcal{V})\rangle>0, where \mathcal{C}(\Psi)=\mathcal{I}_{\max}-\mathcal{I}(\Psi) is the capability gap.

Theorem 9.2 (Coherence preservation; conditional). Under the hypotheses of Theorem 4.5 and (SA1)–(SA5) with (H-REM), if \Psi_0 is geometrically coherent with parameters (\gamma_0,K_0,\delta_0), then \Psi(t) remains geometrically coherent for all t\in[0,T] with parameters (\gamma_0',K_0',\delta_0')=(\gamma_0 e^{-CT},K_0+CT,\delta_0 e^{-CT}).

Proof sketch. (1) Spectral gap preserved up to exponential factors under Lipschitz bounds. (2) Ricci curvature lower bound follows from boundedness of metric coefficients. (3) Basin stability follows from Proposition 3.7 (conditional). (4) Sign of \phi preserved under a coupling condition that is not established here. \square

Remark 9.3. Condition (4) is not established unconditionally; the alignment order parameter \phi may change sign under the field dynamics. Theorem 9.2 is therefore conditional on both (H-REM) and (SA5), in addition to (SA1)–(SA4).

10. The Coupled System

10.1 Fractional Coupling

For consistency with the time-fractional mean-field game framework of Camilli–De Maio (2019), all three equations carry the same Caputo derivative:

\begin{aligned}
{}^C_0D_t^\alpha\Psi&=\nabla\cdot(D(\sigma)\nabla\Psi)+\mathcal{G}(\Psi,m)+\Lambda_{\mathcal{A},k}(\Psi),\\
{}^C_0D_t^\alpha m&=\nu\Delta_x m+\kappa\Delta_{\mathrm{hyb}}m+\nabla_x\cdot(m\nabla_x u),\\
{}^C_0D_t^\alpha u&=-\nu\Delta_x u-\kappa\Delta_{\mathrm{hyb}}u+\frac{1}{2}\|\nabla_x u\|^2-\mathcal{F}(\Psi,m),
\end{aligned}
\tag{10.1}

with Neumann boundary conditions, initial conditions (\Psi_0,m_0), and terminal condition u(T)=0 interpreted as a backward fractional problem.

10.2 Existence

Theorem 10.1 (Conditional existence). Under the hypotheses of Theorem 4.5, the Lipschitz and monotonicity assumptions of Camilli–De Maio (2019) on the HJB and FP equations, condition (3.9), and (H-REM)–(SA5), the coupled system (10.1) admits a mild solution (\Psi,m,u) in the product space

\mathcal{X}=C([0,T];H^1)\times L^2(0,T;\mathcal{P}(\Omega\times\Theta))\times C^{1,2}([0,T]\times\Omega\times\Theta).

Proof sketch. Construct a fixed-point operator \mathcal{T}:\mathcal{X}\to\mathcal{X} by solving the three equations sequentially. The fractional field equation has a solution by Theorem 4.5. The time-fractional HJB and FP equations have solutions by Camilli–De Maio. Alignment projection preserves the invariant convex set under (H-REM) and (SA5). Compactness of \mathcal{T} in the stated topology follows from the regularity estimates of Theorem 4.3 and standard compactness arguments; the details are left to the reader. Apply Schauder–Tychonoff. \square

Remark 10.2. The compactness and continuity of \mathcal{T} in \mathcal{X} are not established in detail here. The fractional Camilli–De Maio theory provides well-posedness of the FP/HJB subsystem, but the required compactness in the chosen topology must be verified separately.

10.3 Convergence to Alignment-Stable NESS

Theorem 10.3 (Conditional convergence). Under the Lyapunov functional \mathcal{E}(t)=\tfrac{1}{2}\|\Psi\|_{L^2}^2+\tfrac{1}{2}\|\nabla\Psi\|_{L^2}^2+\lambda\mathcal{A}_\mu(\Psi,\mathcal{V}), together with (H-REM), (SA4), (SA5), and the fractional Barbălat lemma in the Caputo setting (which we do not establish here), the time-dependent solution (\Psi(t),m(t)) converges strongly in H^1\times L^1 to a non-equilibrium steady state (\Psi^*,m^*) with \mathcal{A}_\mu(\Psi^*,\mathcal{V})\le\epsilon.

