Appendix A: The Fractional Lyapunov Inequality — Statement, Proof, and Scope

This appendix provides the finite-dimensional statement and proof of the fractional Lyapunov inequality, discusses the Hilbert-space extension, and states precisely why the inequality does not apply to the alignment functional of this paper.

A.1 Finite-Dimensional Statement

Theorem A.1 (Gomoyunov, 2017; independently Chen et al., 2017). Let \alpha\in(0,1), T>0, and let V:\mathbb{R}^n\to\mathbb{R} be convex and continuously differentiable. Let x:[0,T]\to\mathbb{R}^n be absolutely continuous with {}^C_0D_t^\alpha x\in L^1(0,T;\mathbb{R}^n). Then for almost every t\in(0,T),

{}^C_0D_t^\alpha V(x(t))\le\left\langle\nabla V(x(t)),\,{}^C_0D_t^\alpha x(t)\right\rangle.
\tag{A.1}

A.2 Proof

We use the alternative representation of the Caputo derivative valid for f\in AC([0,T]):

{}^C_0D_t^\alpha f(t)=\frac{1}{\Gamma(1-\alpha)}\frac{f(t)-f(0)}{t^\alpha}+\frac{\alpha}{\Gamma(1-\alpha)}\int_0^t\frac{f(t)-f(s)}{(t-s)^{1+\alpha}}\,ds.
\tag{A.2}

This follows from {}^C_0D_t^\alpha f={}_0I_t^{1-\alpha}f' and integration by parts. Apply (A.2) to f(t)=V(x(t)):

{}^C_0D_t^\alpha V(x(t))=\frac{1}{\Gamma(1-\alpha)}\frac{V(x(t))-V(x(0))}{t^\alpha}+\frac{\alpha}{\Gamma(1-\alpha)}\int_0^t\frac{V(x(t))-V(x(s))}{(t-s)^{1+\alpha}}\,ds.
\tag{A.3}

Apply (A.2) componentwise to x and take the inner product with \nabla V(x(t)):

\left\langle\nabla V(x(t)),\,{}^C_0D_t^\alpha x(t)\right\rangle=\frac{1}{\Gamma(1-\alpha)}\frac{\langle\nabla V(x(t)),x(t)-x(0)\rangle}{t^\alpha}+\frac{\alpha}{\Gamma(1-\alpha)}\int_0^t\frac{\langle\nabla V(x(t)),x(t)-x(s)\rangle}{(t-s)^{1+\alpha}}\,ds.
\tag{A.4}

By convexity of V, for every s\in[0,t],

V(x(t))-V(x(s))\le\langle\nabla V(x(t)),x(t)-x(s)\rangle.
\tag{A.5}

Substituting (A.5) into the integrands of (A.3) and comparing term-by-term with (A.4) yields (A.1). The boundary term V(x(t))-V(x(0)) is likewise bounded by \langle\nabla V(x(t)),x(t)-x(0)\rangle by the same convexity inequality with s=0. \square

A.3 Hilbert-Space Extension — Status

The extension of Theorem A.1 to functionals on H^1(\Omega\times\Theta) requires a Galerkin argument. We state the result and record its precise status.

Theorem A.2 (Hilbert-space fractional Lyapunov inequality; conditional). Let \mathcal{F}:H^1(\Omega\times\Theta)\to\mathbb{R} be convex and Fréchet differentiable with gradient Lipschitz in L^2(\Omega\times\Theta). Let \Psi\in C([0,T];H^1)\cap C^1((0,T];L^2) with {}^C_0D_t^\alpha\Psi\in L^1(0,T;L^2). Then for almost every t\in(0,T),

{}^C_0D_t^\alpha\mathcal{F}(\Psi(t))\le\left\langle\frac{\delta\mathcal{F}}{\delta\Psi}(\Psi(t)),\,{}^C_0D_t^\alpha\Psi(t)\right\rangle.
\tag{A.6}

Proof strategy (not a complete proof). Let \{e_k\}_{k\ge 1}\subset H^1 be an orthonormal basis of L^2. Let V_N=\mathrm{span}\{e_1,\ldots,e_N\}, \Psi_N(t)=P_N\Psi(t) the Galerkin projection, and \mathcal{F}_N=\mathcal{F}|_{V_N}. Then \mathcal{F}_N:V_N\cong\mathbb{R}^N\to\mathbb{R} is convex and C^1, and \Psi_N satisfies the regularity hypotheses of Theorem A.1. Applying (A.1) to each pair (\mathcal{F}_N,\Psi_N),

{}^C_0D_t^\alpha\mathcal{F}_N(\Psi_N(t))\le\left\langle\nabla\mathcal{F}_N(\Psi_N(t)),\,{}^C_0D_t^\alpha\Psi_N(t)\right\rangle.
\tag{A.7}

Passing to the limit as N\to\infty requires:

(i) \Psi_N\to\Psi in C([0,T];H^1)\cap C^1((0,T];L^2);

(ii) \mathcal{F}_N(\Psi_N)\to\mathcal{F}(\Psi) in C([0,T];\mathbb{R});

(iii) continuity of {}^C_0D_t^\alpha as a bounded operator from C^1((0,T];L^2)\cap L^1(0,T;L^2) to L^1(0,T;L^2);

(iv) convergence of the right-hand side of (A.7) to the right-hand side of (A.6) in L^1(0,T).

Each of (i)–(iv) requires assumptions beyond those stated: (i) requires uniform H^1-boundedness of \Psi_N, (ii) requires \mathcal{F} to be continuous in the strong H^1 topology on bounded sets, and (iv) requires strong L^2-convergence of \nabla\mathcal{F}_N(\Psi_N) to \delta\mathcal{F}/\delta\Psi(\Psi). These are standard but not automatic for Caputo dynamics, and a complete proof does not appear in the cited finite-dimensional sources.

Status. Theorem A.2 is conditional. It is not "established via Galerkin" in the sense of a complete proof; it is a proof scheme whose hypotheses must be verified case-by-case. In this paper we do not require Theorem A.2 in full generality: the alignment functional is not convex (Appendix B), so the inequality does not apply to it in any case.

A.4 Applicability to the Alignment Functional — Refuted

Lemma A.3 (False in general). The alignment functional \mathcal{A}(\Psi,\mathcal{V}) of Definition 3.1 is convex in \Psi on H^1.

Counterexample (Proposition 3.5, restated). Take \Theta=\{1,2\} with counting measure, \mathcal{V} uniform, \Omega a single point, and w\equiv 1. Write \Psi=(a,b). Then

\mathcal{A}(a,b)=\log 2+\frac{a\log a+b\log b}{a+b}-\log(a+b).

At (a,b)=(2,1), the Hessian

H=\begin{pmatrix}0.0043&-0.0854\\-0.0854&0.3249\end{pmatrix}

has determinant 0.0043\cdot 0.3249-0.0854^2=-0.0059<0, so \mathcal{A} is not convex. The source of non-convexity is the normalization \Psi\mapsto\Psi/\rho, which is homogeneous of degree zero, not affine.

Consequence for the framework. The fractional Lyapunov inequality cannot be applied to \mathcal{A} or its regularized form \mathcal{A}_\mu. Hypothesis (H-AI) of earlier revisions is not eliminated; it is replaced by the more precise hypotheses (SA4), (SA5), and (H-REM) stated in the main text.

