Appendix C: Non-Convexity of the Alignment Functional

This appendix records the counterexample to convexity in full detail and draws out its consequences for the applicability of the fractional Lyapunov inequality.

C.1 The Counterexample

Proposition C.1. Let \Theta=\{1,2\} with counting measure \mu_\Theta, \mathcal{V}=(1/2,1/2), \Omega a single point, and w\equiv 1. For \Psi=(a,b) with a,b>0,

\mathcal{A}(a,b)=\log 2+\frac{a\log a+b\log b}{a+b}-\log(a+b).
\tag{C.1}

Proof. By direct computation,

p=\left(\frac{a}{a+b},\frac{b}{a+b}\right),\qquad D_{\mathrm{KL}}(p\|\mathcal{V})=\sum_{i\in\{1,2\}}p_i\log\frac{p_i}{1/2}=\sum_i p_i\log p_i+\log 2.

Substituting p_1=a/(a+b), p_2=b/(a+b) gives (C.1). \square

C.2 Hessian Computation

Write s=a+b and denote \mathcal{A}(a,b)=\log 2+\phi(a,b)-\log s with \phi=(a\log a+b\log b)/s. The first derivatives are

\partial_a\phi=\frac{\log a+1-\phi}{s},\qquad\partial_b\phi=\frac{\log b+1-\phi}{s}.

The second derivatives are

\partial_{aa}\phi=\frac{1}{as}-\frac{2(\log a+1-\phi)}{s^2},\qquad\partial_{bb}\phi=\frac{1}{bs}-\frac{2(\log b+1-\phi)}{s^2},

\partial_{ab}\phi=-\frac{\log a+1-\phi}{s^2}-\frac{\log b+1-\phi}{s^2}.

Adding the contribution -\partial_{ij}\log s with \partial_{aa}\log s=\partial_{bb}\log s=-1/s^2, \partial_{ab}\log s=1/s^2:

\partial_{aa}\mathcal{A}=\frac{1}{as}-\frac{2(\log a+1-\phi)}{s^2}+\frac{1}{s^2},\qquad
\partial_{bb}\mathcal{A}=\frac{1}{bs}-\frac{2(\log b+1-\phi)}{s^2}+\frac{1}{s^2},

\partial_{ab}\mathcal{A}=-\frac{\log a+\log b+2-2\phi}{s^2}-\frac{1}{s^2}.

At (a,b)=(2,1): s=3, \phi=(2\log 2+0)/3=2\log 2/3\approx 0.4621. Numerically,

H=\begin{pmatrix}0.0043&-0.0854\\-0.0854&0.3249\end{pmatrix},\qquad \det H=-0.0059<0.

A negative determinant of the Hessian at a point implies the function is not convex on any neighborhood of that point. \square

C.3 Structural Origin of Non-Convexity

The map \Psi\mapsto p=\Psi/\rho is homogeneous of degree zero: p_{\lambda\Psi}=p_\Psi for all \lambda>0. Composition of a degree-zero homogeneous map with the convex function D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\cdot\|\mathcal{V}) does not preserve convexity. Convexity is preserved under composition with affine maps; homogeneity of degree zero is incompatible with affinity except in degenerate cases.

C.4 Consequences for the Framework

1. The fractional Lyapunov inequality (Theorem A.1, Theorem A.2) does not apply to \mathcal{A} or \mathcal{A}_\mu.
2. Hypothesis (H-AI) of earlier revisions is not eliminated.
3. Alignment invariance (Proposition 3.7 of the main text) must be stated with the explicit gradient-domination hypothesis (SA4) and the fractional-remainder hypothesis (H-REM).

We remark that the negative entropy functional

\mathcal{B}(\Psi)=\int_{\Omega\times\Theta}\Psi\log\frac{\Psi}{\rho}\,d(\mathbf{x},\theta)

is convex in \Psi, since it is the integral of \Psi times the logarithm of the conditional density, which is convex on the cone of non-negative functions. However, \mathcal{B} is not equal to \mathcal{A}, and the sublevel set \{\mathcal{A}\le\epsilon\} is not a sublevel set of \mathcal{B}. Replacing \mathcal{A} by \mathcal{B} would change the alignment constraint and is not a reformulation of the present framework.

