Appendix D: Diffusion Dissipation — Invalid Chain Rule and Corrected Formulation

This appendix documents the invalid pointwise chain rule used in earlier revisions and states the corrected formulation as an explicit hypothesis.

D.1 The Invalid Identity

Earlier revisions asserted

\nabla\!\left(\frac{\delta\mathcal{A}}{\delta\Psi}\right)=\frac{\delta^2\mathcal{A}}{\delta\Psi^2}\nabla\Psi,
\tag{D.1}

treating \delta\mathcal{A}/\delta\Psi as a pointwise function of \Psi. This is invalid: \delta\mathcal{A}/\delta\Psi(\mathbf{x},\theta) depends on \Psi nonlocally through

(i) the normalization \rho(\mathbf{x})=\int_\Theta\Psi\,d\mu_\Theta, which depends on \Psi at all \theta'\in\Theta;

(ii) the KL divergence D_{\mathrm{KL}}(p_{\mathbf{x}}\|\mathcal{V}), which depends on the full distribution p_{\mathbf{x}} at \mathbf{x}.

Consequently, the second functional derivative \delta^2\mathcal{A}/\delta\Psi^2 is a two-point kernel K(\mathbf{x},\theta;\mathbf{x}',\theta'), and \nabla(\delta\mathcal{A}/\delta\Psi) is an integral against this kernel, not a pointwise multiplication.

D.2 Correct Expression for the Diffusion Contribution

Integration by parts using Neumann boundary conditions gives

\left\langle\frac{\delta\mathcal{A}_\mu}{\delta\Psi},\,\nabla\cdot(D\nabla\Psi)\right\rangle=-\int_{\Omega\times\Theta}D\,\nabla\Psi\cdot\nabla\!\left(\frac{\delta\mathcal{A}_\mu}{\delta\Psi}\right)d(\mathbf{x},\theta).
\tag{D.2}

This is exact. The sign of the right-hand side is not determined by convexity of \mathcal{A}_\mu (which fails; Appendix C) nor by convexity of D (which is not assumed).

D.3 Partial Computation Under Simplifying Assumptions

Assume \mathcal{V} constant in \theta, w\equiv\text{const}, and \Psi>0. From (B.10),

\frac{\delta\mathcal{A}_\mu}{\delta\Psi}=\frac{w}{\rho_\mu}\left[\log\frac{p^{(\mu)}}{\mathcal{V}_\mu}-D_{\mathrm{KL}}\right].

Taking the gradient in \mathbf{x},

\nabla\!\left(\frac{\delta\mathcal{A}_\mu}{\delta\Psi}\right)=\frac{w}{\rho_\mu}\left[\frac{\nabla\Psi}{\Psi+\mu}-\frac{\nabla\rho_\mu}{\rho_\mu}\log\frac{p^{(\mu)}}{\mathcal{V}_\mu}\right]-\frac{w\nabla\rho_\mu}{\rho_\mu^2}\left[\log\frac{p^{(\mu)}}{\mathcal{V}_\mu}-D_{\mathrm{KL}}\right].
\tag{D.3}

The inner product with \nabla\Psi contains terms of both signs; no general non-positivity follows.

D.4 The Hypothesis (SA5)

We impose (SA5) as an explicit hypothesis:

\left\langle\frac{\delta\mathcal{A}_\mu}{\delta\Psi},\,\nabla\cdot(D\nabla\Psi)\right\rangle\le\kappa_{\mathrm{d}}\left\|\frac{\delta\mathcal{A}_\mu}{\delta\Psi}\right\|_{L^2}^2.
\tag{D.4}

The case \kappa_{\mathrm{d}}=0 would be unconditional dissipativity. The partial computation in §D.3 suggests that \kappa_{\mathrm{d}}>0 in general, but a proof of the bound (D.4) with an explicit constant is not available.

D.5 Consequences

Under (SA5), the diffusion contribution to {}^C_0D_t^\alpha\mathcal{A}_\mu is bounded above by \kappa_{\mathrm{d}}\|\delta\mathcal{A}_\mu/\delta\Psi\|_{L^2}^2. This enters the dissipation chain of Appendix E; the constant \kappa_{\mathrm{d}} must be dominated by the projection gain c_0=\eta/2, which is achieved by choosing \eta>2\kappa_{\mathrm{d}}.

