Appendix E: Reformulation of Alignment Invariance

This appendix assembles the conditional chain of inequalities leading to Proposition 3.7 of the main text, and identifies each hypothesis that enters.

E.1 Fractional Leibniz Representation

For \mathcal{A}_\mu sufficiently regular in time (e.g., \mathcal{A}_\mu(\Psi(\cdot),\mathcal{V})\in AC([0,T]) with {}^C_0D_t^\alpha\mathcal{A}_\mu\in L^1(0,T)) and \Psi in the class of Theorem 4.4, the fractional Leibniz rule gives

{}^C_0D_t^\alpha\mathcal{A}_\mu(\Psi(t),\mathcal{V})=\left\langle\frac{\delta\mathcal{A}_\mu}{\delta\Psi}(\Psi(t)),\,{}^C_0D_t^\alpha\Psi(t)\right\rangle+R_\mu(t),
\tag{E.1}

where R_\mu(t) is the fractional Leibniz remainder. For convex \mathcal{A}_\mu the Gomoyunov inequality would give R_\mu\le 0. For non-convex \mathcal{A}_\mu the sign of R_\mu is not determined in general.

E.2 Hypothesis (H-REM)

We impose

R_\mu(t)\le 0\quad\text{for a.e. }t\in(0,T),\text{ along solutions of (3.1) in the class of Theorem 4.4}.
\tag{H-REM}

E.3 Substitution of the Field Equation

Substituting (3.1) into (E.1) with (H-REM),

{}^C_0D_t^\alpha\mathcal{A}_\mu\le\underbrace{\left\langle\frac{\delta\mathcal{A}_\mu}{\delta\Psi},\,\nabla\cdot(D\nabla\Psi)\right\rangle}_{(\mathrm{i})}+\underbrace{\left\langle\frac{\delta\mathcal{A}_\mu}{\delta\Psi},\,F_{\mathrm{BUDT}}\right\rangle}_{(\mathrm{ii})}+\underbrace{\left\langle\frac{\delta\mathcal{A}_\mu}{\delta\Psi},\,\Lambda_{\mathcal{A},k}\right\rangle}_{(\mathrm{iii})}.
\tag{E.2}

(i) Diffusion term. Under (SA5): (\mathrm{i})\le\kappa_{\mathrm{d}}\|\delta\mathcal{A}_\mu/\delta\Psi\|_{L^2}^2.

(ii) Reaction term. Under the alignment-compatibility condition (3.9): (\mathrm{ii})\le 0 on \{\mathcal{A}_\mu>\epsilon\}.

(iii) Projection term. By the chain rule for \Lambda_{\mathcal{A},k}=-\eta\sigma_k'(\mathcal{A}_\mu-\epsilon)\delta\mathcal{A}_\mu/\delta\Psi,

(\mathrm{iii})=-\eta\sigma_k'(\mathcal{A}_\mu-\epsilon)\left\|\frac{\delta\mathcal{A}_\mu}{\delta\Psi}\right\|_{L^2}^2\le-c_0\left\|\frac{\delta\mathcal{A}_\mu}{\delta\Psi}\right\|_{L^2}^2
\tag{E.3}

on \{\mathcal{A}_\mu>\epsilon\}, with c_0=\eta/2 (since \sigma_k'(z)\ge 1/2 for z\ge 0).

E.4 Differential Inequality

Combining (E.2)–(E.3),

{}^C_0D_t^\alpha\mathcal{A}_\mu\le-(c_0-\kappa_{\mathrm{d}})\left\|\frac{\delta\mathcal{A}_\mu}{\delta\Psi}\right\|_{L^2}^2
\tag{E.4}

on \{\mathcal{A}_\mu>\epsilon\}. Under (SA4) with exponent \beta\in(0,1),

{}^C_0D_t^\alpha\mathcal{A}_\mu\le-c\,(\mathcal{A}_\mu-\epsilon)_+^{2\beta},\qquad c=(c_0-\kappa_{\mathrm{d}})c_{\mathrm{L}}>0,
\tag{E.5}

provided \eta>2\kappa_{\mathrm{d}}.

E.5 Comparison Argument

Proposition E.1 (Alignment invariance; conditional). Suppose (SA1)–(SA5), (H-REM), (3.9), and \eta>2\kappa_{\mathrm{d}}. If \mathcal{A}_\mu(\Psi_0,\mathcal{V})\le\epsilon, then \mathcal{A}_\mu(\Psi(t),\mathcal{V})\le\epsilon for all t\in[0,T].

Proof. Apply the fractional comparison principle to (E.5). The function f(t,y)=-c(y-\epsilon)_+^{2\beta} is continuous and Lipschitz in y for \beta\in(0,1) (the map y\mapsto(y-\epsilon)_+^{2\beta} is Hölder, and its composition with the linear factor y-\epsilon is Lipschitz on bounded sets). The constant function y(t)\equiv\epsilon satisfies {}^C_0D_t^\alpha y=0\ge f(t,\epsilon)=0 and y(0)=\epsilon\ge\mathcal{A}_\mu(\Psi_0,\mathcal{V}). Lemma 4.1 (main text) gives \mathcal{A}_\mu(\Psi(t),\mathcal{V})\le\epsilon for all t\in[0,T]. \square

E.6 Constant Dependence on \mu

The constants c_{\mathrm{L}} in (SA4), \kappa_{\mathrm{d}} in (SA5), and c_0 in (E.3) all depend on the regularization parameter \mu_{\min}. As \mu_{\min}\to 0, c_{\mathrm{L}} may degenerate (the Łojasiewicz constant is expected to scale as a power of \delta_{\min} in the unregularized case) and \kappa_{\mathrm{d}} may grow. The invariance statement should therefore be read at fixed \mu_{\min}>0.

