Appendix G: Maximal Regularity and Sectoriality

This appendix records the precise form of the maximal-regularity theorem used and the sectoriality properties of the quasilinear diffusion operator.

G.1 Maximal Regularity for Subdiffusion

We use the subdiffusion maximal-regularity theory of Zacher (2012), which is stated for the Caputo derivative and the quasilinear problem

{}^C_0D_t^\alpha(u-u_0)+A(u)u=f(u)+h(t),\qquad u(0)=u_0.
\tag{G.1}

The interpolation-space framework follows Clément–Londen–Simonett (2004), whose fractional-order theory is stated for parabolic operators (with \alpha=1 recovering the classical parabolic problem). Their results are used here for the associated parabolic problem A(\Psi) at each fixed \Psi; the subdiffusion extension is Zacher's. The two sources are complementary and should not be conflated.

Theorem G.1 (Zacher, 2012). Let X_0,X_1 be Banach spaces with X_1\hookrightarrow X_0 densely. Let A(\cdot):X_1\to X_0 be a family of closed operators such that -A(u) generates an analytic semigroup on X_0 for each u\in X_1, uniformly sectorial. Under Lipschitz and continuity assumptions on A(\cdot) and f(\cdot) in the interpolation space X_\gamma=(X_0,X_1)_{\gamma,\infty} for suitable \gamma, there exist T_0>0 and a unique solution

u\in C([0,T_0];X_\gamma)\cap C((0,T_0];X_1),\qquad{}^C_0D_t^\alpha u\in C((0,T_0];X_0).

If the solution remains bounded in X_\gamma for all finite times, it extends globally.

G.2 Uniform Sectoriality

Lemma G.2. Let D:\Omega\times\Theta\times[0,T]\to[D_{\min},D_{\max}] be measurable with 0<D_{\min}\le D_{\max}<\infty, and define

A=-\nabla\cdot(D\nabla\cdot):H^2_N(\Omega\times\Theta)\to L^2(\Omega\times\Theta).
\tag{G.2}

Then -A generates an analytic semigroup on L^2 with sector \Sigma_\theta=\{\lambda\in\mathbb{C}:|\arg\lambda|<\pi-\theta\} for \theta\in(0,\pi/2), and \|(\lambda+A)^{-1}\|_{L(L^2)}\le M/|\lambda| on \Sigma_\theta with M depending only on D_{\min},D_{\max} and the domain.

Proof. The operator A is self-adjoint and positive on L^2 with form domain H^1 and form

a(u,v)=\int_{\Omega\times\Theta}D\,\nabla u\cdot\nabla v\,d(\mathbf{x},\theta).
\tag{G.3}

Uniform ellipticity D\ge D_{\min} gives coercivity; D\le D_{\max} gives boundedness. The spectral theorem yields the resolvent bound with M=1. \square

G.3 Lipschitz Dependence on \Psi

(SA3) as stated in the main text: D(\mathbf{x},\theta,t)=\Phi(\sigma(\mathbf{x},\theta,t)) with \Phi:\mathbb{R}\to[D_{\min},D_{\max}] Lipschitz and \sigma:[0,T]\to H^s(\Omega\times\Theta) Lipschitz from H^1 to H^s on bounded subsets, for some s>d/2. This ensures H^s\hookrightarrow L^\infty and gives the Lipschitz estimate

\|D(\Psi_1)-D(\Psi_2)\|_{L^\infty}\le L_\Phi L_\sigma\|\Psi_1-\Psi_2\|_{H^1}.
\tag{G.4}

For u\in H^2_N,

\|(A(\Psi_1)-A(\Psi_2))u\|_{L^2}\le C(R)\|\Psi_1-\Psi_2\|_{H^1}\|u\|_{H^2}
\tag{G.5}

for \|\Psi_i\|_{H^1}\le R, using the embedding H^s\hookrightarrow L^\infty and the Gagliardo–Nirenberg inequality.

G.4 Remark on Dimensions

In dimensions d\le 2, H^1\hookrightarrow L^\infty and the hypothesis s>d/2 is satisfied with s=1. In dimensions d\ge 3, H^1\not\hookrightarrow L^\infty, and the hypothesis s>d/2 is a genuine restriction. Earlier revisions stated (SA3) with H^1 Lipschitz dependence without qualification; the corrected version requires H^s with s>d/2.

