Appendix H: Well-Definedness of the Hybrid Fisher–WFR Metric

H.1 Fisher–Rao Metric

Let \mathcal{M}\subset\mathcal{P}(\Theta) be a parameterized family \{p_\theta\}_{\theta\in\Theta_{\mathcal{M}}} with \Theta_{\mathcal{M}}\subset\mathbb{R}^m open. The Fisher information matrix is

g^{\mathrm{Fisher}}_{ij}(\theta)=\int_\Theta\partial_i\log p_\theta\,\partial_j\log p_\theta\,p_\theta\,d\mu_\Theta(\theta).
\tag{H.1}

It is symmetric and positive semi-definite, possibly degenerate.

H.2 WFR Metric

The WFR metric on \mathcal{P}(\Theta) is defined via the Benamou–Brenier formulation for unbalanced transport:

g^{\mathrm{WFR}}(\dot{\theta},\dot{\theta})=\inf_{(\mu,v)}\left\{\int_0^1\int_\Theta\left(|v_t|^2+|\nabla\phi_t|^2\right)d\mu_t\,dt\right\},
\tag{H.2}

subject to \partial_t\mu_t+\nabla\cdot(\mu_t v_t)=\mu_t\nabla\phi_t with \mu_0=\mu_1=p_\theta, the infimum over paths in \mathcal{M} tangent to \dot{\theta} at \theta.

H.3 Hypothesis (H-WFR)

The family \mathcal{M} is such that:

(i) the WFR metric restricted to \mathcal{M} is well-defined and finite for all tangent vectors in T_\theta\mathcal{M};

(ii) the WFR metric is positive-definite on \mathcal{M};

(iii) the WFR metric depends smoothly on \theta\in\Theta_{\mathcal{M}}.

Hypothesis (H-WFR) holds for the Gaussian family \mathcal{M}=\{N(m,\Sigma):m\in\mathbb{R}^d,\Sigma\in\mathrm{Sym}^+_d\}, where explicit formulas for both g^{\mathrm{Fisher}} and g^{\mathrm{WFR}} are available. It may fail for degenerate families.

H.4 Positive-Definiteness

Proposition H.1. Under (H-WFR) and \lambda\in(0,1], the hybrid metric

g^{\mathrm{hyb}}_{ij}(\theta)=(1-\lambda)g^{\mathrm{Fisher}}_{ij}(\theta)+\lambda g^{\mathrm{WFR}}_{ij}(\theta)
\tag{H.3}

is positive-definite on \mathcal{M}, even where g^{\mathrm{Fisher}} degenerates.

Proof. For any non-zero v\in T_\theta\mathcal{M},

v^\top g^{\mathrm{hyb}}v=(1-\lambda)v^\top g^{\mathrm{Fisher}}v+\lambda v^\top g^{\mathrm{WFR}}v\ge\lambda v^\top g^{\mathrm{WFR}}v>0
\tag{H.4}

by positive-definiteness of g^{\mathrm{WFR}}. \square

H.5 Natural Gradient Flow

The free energy gradient flow is

\dot{\theta}=-(g^{\mathrm{hyb}})^{-1}\nabla_\theta\mathcal{F}(\theta;o).
\tag{H.5}

Under (H-WFR) and local Lipschitzness of \nabla_\theta\mathcal{F}, Picard–Lindelöf gives a unique local solution for every \theta_0\in\Theta_{\mathcal{M}}.

