Appendix J: Reputation-Aware Mean-Field Games — Citation and Scope

J.1 Citation Status

The reputation-aware mean-field game framework cited in the main text (§8) as "FedMFG" refers to work on federated learning with reputation mechanisms (Sun, Wu, and Li, IEEE Internet of Things Journal, 2024). This is a systems paper on federated learning; it does not contain:

(i) a Nash-equilibrium-in-Lipschitz-controls theorem;

(ii) a spectral-gap analysis of the linearized Fokker–Planck operator;

(iii) the critical-fraction formula \rho_c of (8.1).

The statements in §8 of the main text are therefore conditional on a mean-field game framework that must be supplied separately. They are not consequences of the cited source.

J.2 Conditional Framework

Assume a reputation-aware mean-field game framework satisfying:

(FR-1) existence and uniqueness of Nash equilibrium in a space of Lipschitz controls;

(FR-2) a spectral gap \lambda_{\min}>0 for the linearized coupled FP–HJB operator at the NESS;

(FR-3) a reputation update r^i_{t+1}=r^i_t+\kappa(\cos(a^i_t,\bar{a}_t)-r^i_t) with threshold \theta_r for exclusion.

J.3 Conditional Critical Fraction

Claim J.1 (conditional). Under (FR-1)–(FR-3), if

\rho<\rho_c=\frac{1}{2}\left(1-\frac{\kappa}{\kappa+\lambda_{\min}(D)}\right),
\tag{J.1}

then the alignment divergence at the NESS satisfies

\mathcal{A}(\Psi^*,\mathcal{V})\le\epsilon+C\rho
\tag{J.2}

for a constant C depending on \theta_r and the spectral gap.

Derivation sketch. The reputation mechanism excludes adversarial agents whose reputation falls below \theta_r at rate governed by \kappa. The adversarial contribution to the FP source term is bounded by \rho(1-\theta_r). Balancing against the spectral gap \lambda_{\min}(D) yields

\rho(1-\theta_r)\le\frac{\lambda_{\min}(D)}{\kappa+\lambda_{\min}(D)}\cdot\frac{1}{2}.
\tag{J.3}

Setting \theta_r=0 and rearranging gives (J.1). The exact constants depend on the specific reputation update and the spectral properties of the coupled system, and must be verified against a primary source.

J.4 Diffusion Floor

The floor D\ge D_{\min} in (4.1) ensures \lambda_{\min}(D)\ge\lambda_{\min}(D_{\min})>0, so \rho_c remains bounded below by a positive constant.

