Appendix K: Coupled System and Fixed-Point Argument

K.1 Fractional Coupling

All three equations in (10.1) carry the same Caputo derivative {}^C_0D_t^\alpha with the same \alpha\in(0,1). This is required for consistency with the time-fractional MFG framework of Camilli–De Maio (2019).

K.2 Terminal Condition

The HJB equation carries terminal condition u(T)=0. With a forward Caputo derivative, this is interpreted as a backward fractional problem: solve for u on [0,T] with {}^C_0D_t^\alpha u=\ldots and u(T)=0. Camilli–De Maio handle this via time reversal t\mapsto T-t and the corresponding right-sided Caputo derivative.

K.3 Fixed-Point Argument

Define \mathcal{T}:\mathcal{X}\to\mathcal{X} with \mathcal{X}=C([0,T];H^1)\times L^2(0,T;\mathcal{P}(\Omega\times\Theta))\times C^{1,2}([0,T]\times\Omega\times\Theta) by

\mathcal{T}(\Psi,m,u)=(\Psi',m',u'),
\tag{K.1}

where \Psi' solves the field equation with (m,u) fixed, m' solves the FP equation with (\Psi,u) fixed, and u' solves the HJB equation with (\Psi,m) fixed.

Theorem K.1 (Conditional existence). Under the hypotheses of Theorem 4.5, the Lipschitz and monotonicity assumptions of Camilli–De Maio on the HJB and FP equations, the alignment-compatibility condition (3.9), and (H-REM)–(SA5), the operator \mathcal{T} has a fixed point in \mathcal{X}.

Proof sketch. By Theorem 4.5, the field equation has a unique solution in C([0,T];H^1) for each (m,u). By Camilli–De Maio, the FP and HJB equations have unique solutions. The alignment projection preserves the invariant convex set under (H-REM) and (SA5). Schauder–Tychonoff applies provided \mathcal{T} is compact and continuous in \mathcal{X}.

Remark K.2. Compactness and continuity of \mathcal{T} in \mathcal{X} are not established in detail here. The fractional Camilli–De Maio theory provides well-posedness of the FP/HJB subsystem, but the required compactness in the chosen topology must be verified separately. The statement of Theorem K.1 is conditional on this verification.

K.4 Convergence to NESS

Theorem K.3 (Conditional convergence). Under the Lyapunov functional

\mathcal{E}(t)=\frac{1}{2}\|\Psi\|_{L^2}^2+\frac{1}{2}\|\nabla\Psi\|_{L^2}^2+\lambda\mathcal{A}_\mu(\Psi,\mathcal{V}),
\tag{K.2}

together with (H-REM), (SA4), (SA5), and the fractional Barbălat lemma in the Caputo setting, the solution (\Psi(t),m(t)) converges strongly in H^1\times L^1 to a NESS (\Psi^*,m^*) with \mathcal{A}_\mu(\Psi^*,\mathcal{V})\le\epsilon.

Remark K.4. The fractional Barbălat lemma in the Caputo setting is not established here and is not cited in the bibliography. Its formulation and proof are open problems in the fractional analysis literature.

